WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.330
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We move on, then, to the
next item on the agenda, which is devolution

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.730
and our proposed principles and decision-making
criteria. I am going to invite Ian to introduce

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:16.520
the paper.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.240
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Chair.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:29.720
Devolution is one potential approach to collaboration
between the NHS and local government. It is

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:39.289
consistent with the move to delegate functions
to CCGs and to place-based commissioning and

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:46.410
more integrated provision. It has the potential
to unlock stronger partnership working between

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:54.140
the NHS and local government. Our close working
with Greater Manchester is a very good example

00:00:54.140 --> 00:01:04.640
of that. NHS England has its own very clear
set of statutory duties and responsibilities.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:14.170
Insofar as we consider entering into future
devolution arrangements with other parts of

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:21.070
the country, the Board will naturally want
to assure itself on a number of points.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:30.640
The paper presented today sets out some proposed
principles and decision criteria for how we

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:38.820
would assess propositions coming forward from
local areas. Those criteria include the clarity

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:46.750
of the purpose of the bid; the clarity of
the vision of what the different parties are

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:55.430
seeking to achieve together; and why the bid
is necessary to enable that, rather than merely

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:04.710
utilising existing extensive powers to enter
into joint arrangements. We would expect robust

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:14.340
governance arrangements; clear and effective
collective leadership; a continued commitment

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:21.310
to national standards and a track record of
compliance with them, in relation to both

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:29.640
quality and finance; local support for what
is proposed; and strong relationships with

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.650
the community and, indeed, other communities
with whom the devolved areas will need to

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.879
have an ongoing relationship.

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:48.889
I also ask the Board to recognise the practicalities
of the amount of time it takes to move from

00:02:48.889 --> 00:02:55.810
early discussions around the principles into
worked-up agreements. Our experience with

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.599
Greater Manchester suggests that this is likely
to be in the order of 18 months. I invite

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:11.939
the Board to consider the principles and decision-making
criteria and, in the light of that, resolve

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:17.409
to agree that the Commissioning Committee
of the Board oversees any future decisions

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:19.430
around entering into arrangements.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.279
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ian. Let me invite
comments and questions from members of the

00:03:24.279 --> 00:03:24.959
Board.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:36.609
&gt;&gt;Ciaran Devane: To be successful, there is
going to be a different model of operation.

00:03:36.609 --> 00:03:44.079
Some of that will be hard skills like population
evidence, analytics, and truly understanding

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:48.629
what is happening with the pathways. Some
of it will be soft, around the ability to

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:53.450
collaborate as opposed to compete, which some
of the geographies going for devolution have

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.319
been quite good at. What are we going to do
in order to have interventions which ask if

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:03.349
we are building the skills, both hard and
soft, to make this work?

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:12.799
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: If I may, can I turn to Paul
to pick up on what is being done in relation

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:17.799
to Greater Manchester, which is our current
live example? I am the Board lead for devolution

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:19.180
more widely, and Paul has been leading the
work on Greater Manchester.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:24.680
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: I am not sure it directly
addresses your question because, to the point

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:31.810
of the criteria, in Manchester we identified
a set of leaders who had a strong track record

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:37.379
of doing collaboration and who could build
on that in the ambitious plans which we have

00:04:37.379 --> 00:04:43.889
been pursuing there. So whilst we continue
to give lots of support to the Manchester

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:48.620
team in doing all of that, the starting point
was not one where there was a massive need

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.750
for development of leadership capability in
the sorts of areas you are talking about.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:58.090
Most of the support has gone into the harder
stuff that you talk about, which is perhaps

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:04.110
not at the centre of your question. I think
that is why, in a sense, we cannot afford

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:09.940
to take on devolution cases where there is
not already a strong collaborative leadership

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.370
track record, which is one of the principles
we have got here.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:21.539
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: If I may than add to Paul's comment,
I think your question highlights a need, across

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:29.740
the Health Service, for us to look to nurture
and develop collaborative leadership styles

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:36.250
and models. I think that is a theme that is
picked up in Ed's review, and something that

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.409
we need to progress as we implement that review.

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:45.220
&gt;&gt;Ciaran Devane: And the analytical side,
and population evidence and new models of

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.620
commissioning?

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:54.720
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Through a combination of different
programmes that we have - so, not specifically

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:04.409
in relation to devolution - we are looking
to improve the basics of business intelligence

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:13.169
for commissioners through things like the
Lead Provider framework and the work on information

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:18.979
for commissioners. It is vital that we get
that right, and I think there is a combination

00:06:18.979 --> 00:06:25.500
of three elements to it. One is improving
the calibre of the data sources and the timeliness

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.120
of the data, and resolving some of the long-standing
information governance constraints. Secondly,

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:42.189
there is the nature of the analytical tools
themselves. Third is the skills for people

00:06:42.189 --> 00:06:49.169
to be able to make best use of those tools;
because, to pick up on your point of population

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:59.449
health analytics, a lot of the best tools
are intelligent query tools that are available

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:06.400
for curious users to work out what this means
for different questions they may have. So

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.240
simply constructing the tools and making them
available will be inadequate in terms of achieving

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.069
the effect that we want.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.520
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: A question first, Malcolm, about
principle three: the principle of subsidiarity

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:28.300
and ensuring that decisions are made at the
most appropriate level. Is it not the lowest

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.990
appropriate level, this subsidiarity? Should
we not have that in there?

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:38.080
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Yes. I think that is a very helpful
clarification, Moira.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.930
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Secondly, have we discussed
these principles with local government? Will

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:51.259
they land well with them, or will they be
seen as a defence against devolution?

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:56.400
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I can confirm there have been
conversations with local government.

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:58.039
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Will it land well?

00:07:58.039 --> 00:08:06.979
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I think that we, in NHS England
and with CCGs, are highly supportive of how

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:13.770
we make devolution work. These principles
are intended to achieve that goal, rather

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:20.280
than to act as a barrier to different forms
of collaboration, of which MOUs between the

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.169
NHS and local government are one type.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:29.509
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think this is going to
take different forms in different parts of

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:34.840
the country, Moira. Obviously Greater Manchester
is in its own category, for the reasons that

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:38.940
Paul has set out - not just because it is
first off the blocks, but because a lot of

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:43.039
the heavy lifting that would need to be done
to make it successful was already a work in

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:46.820
progress. Different parts of the country are
coming up with different propositions. In

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.130
my Board report, you saw the work that the
local authorities in the West Midlands are

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.639
wanting to do on mental health and work, and
they are looking to us for support specifically

00:08:54.639 --> 00:09:00.010
around that agenda. We will play in those,
but above all what I think local government

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:07.380
is looking for from us is a clearly understandable,
predictable point of view about the circumstances

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:12.380
under which we will and will not engage with
different models or propositions. I think

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:18.220
that, actually, the devolution label sometimes
obscures rather than reveals, because in the

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:26.010
context of the ten councils in Greater Manchester,
what we are actually doing is three separate

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:32.130
things together. We are devolving some of
the national budgets, they are aggregating

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:37.040
some of the fragmented local budgets, and
we are blending health and social care. So

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:44.120
actually it is a devolution, an aggregation
and a blending, not just a 'Devo Manc'.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:53.260
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Some of these criteria
are quite difficult to measure and judge,

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:58.829
and we have not done this before. I am curious
about whether and how we are going to learn

00:09:58.829 --> 00:10:03.190
as we go along. For instance, 'Demonstrable
track record of collaboration and engagement

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:10.380
with patients and local communities'. Are
we going to be looking for evidence that they

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:17.220
are engaging with NHS citizens? There are
really two questions: are we learning as we

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:24.230
go along, and do we have sub-criteria or measures
that we are using to make these judgements?

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:28.740
It is kind of new - for us, as well.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:34.690
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: My answer partly reflects your
last comment, Victor. This is new for us,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:42.589
so these are a set of high-level principles.
There is no sub-level of different, clear

00:10:42.589 --> 00:10:49.399
metrics that we have that we can apply today
in relation to each of these areas. There

00:10:49.399 --> 00:10:55.199
is a strong element of judgement in relation
to each. That said, with some of the criteria,

00:10:55.199 --> 00:11:01.019
such as current operational performance or
financial performance, it is much easier to

00:11:01.019 --> 00:11:05.000
look at the existing quantitative information.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:15.760
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think the important factor
in this is that this is not saying, 'Here

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:22.500
is our plan. Agree it or don't.' Actually,
this is an iteration and a conversation. A

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:33.199
number of these principles are things that
we would want to develop with the areas concerned,

00:11:33.199 --> 00:11:38.389
rather than be presented with. A number of
them, such as a clear mitigation plan, will

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:44.329
clearly come out of the experience that we
have had from doing other things. My sense

00:11:44.329 --> 00:11:49.990
is that we have to be quite adept at learning
from the various experiences, and also helping

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:56.029
to shape some of the proposals based on the
learning that we get. Therefore, Ian, the

00:11:56.029 --> 00:11:59.370
question is whether we have the capability
and capacity to do that, given that there

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:00.940
is significant interest.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:06.850
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Your framing of the issue is
extremely helpful, David. What we do not want

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:12.060
to do is to see this as a single, episodic
gate.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:12.880
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Correct.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:17.899
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Instead, this is part of the
development of the relationship between the

00:12:17.899 --> 00:12:26.740
NHS and local government more widely. My understanding
is that there has been and is considerable

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:33.240
engagement between different NHS partners,
locally at CCG level and with area teams with

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:38.199
local government, as people have been putting
together their propositions. Inevitably, given

00:12:38.199 --> 00:12:47.550
the tightness of the Treasury deadlines for
September, the propositions that come in will

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:53.459
in some instances be staging posts rather
than the final word. I do not know whether,

00:12:53.459 --> 00:12:59.410
Barbara, you want to add anything from the
perspective of your regional director or director

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:05.019
of commissioning operations colleagues on
how the relationships with local government

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:07.220
are going on those propositions?

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:13.250
&gt;&gt;Barbara Hakin: I think we have seen a significant
improvement in the relationships across the

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:19.690
board with the Better Care Fund. The Better
Care Fund has drawn together CCG leaders,

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:25.980
our leaders and local government leaders to
work on the whole health and social care integration

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:34.199
agenda. Across the country, people are building
on that, particularly where there is a strong

00:13:34.199 --> 00:13:39.699
interest in devolution. We are seeing people
taking about it, and I think people are seeing

00:13:39.699 --> 00:13:46.009
it as a shared initiative. As ever with all
these things, it comes down to personalities.

00:13:46.009 --> 00:13:52.800
You can see places where the leaders are absolutely
committed to working together, and have been

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.860
before the idea of devolution. They are the
ones who can make the most rapid progress,

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:01.750
but we do still have some areas where it seems
that personalities get in the way of progress.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:06.269
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think that is important.
Moira made the point before about whether

00:14:06.269 --> 00:14:13.600
these are a set of criteria that are impossible
to deliver, or a clear set of staging-posts

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:23.279
that people can see. I do not think we should
see these as being cast in stone, such that

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:30.589
people have be perfect on all of them. On
the other hand, we absolutely need colleagues

00:14:30.589 --> 00:14:35.490
in local government to recognise that actually
this is born out of our experience and that,

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:40.319
unless you get these things right, you are
going to find it difficult. In the words of

00:14:40.319 --> 00:14:47.110
one my ex-bosses, who was very wise: from
those to whom much is given, much is expected.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:53.880
Therefore, we can be a facilitator and an
enabler to help make this work, and I think

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:57.930
that will require both the local government
side and ourselves to have a slightly different

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:01.009
mindset than we might have had before.

00:15:01.009 --> 00:15:08.040
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think that has been very
valuable, Ian. The social geography and physical

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:13.620
geography of England is terribly varied - great
demographic differences and great leadership

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:20.970
differences. What we are doing here, I think,
is recognising that; not from a top-down perspective

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:28.910
but in trying to understand and accommodate
bids for local self-determination, of which

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:36.009
health is but one part of a broader package.
I think the Board are telling you that the

00:15:36.009 --> 00:15:41.170
criteria that you have specified in the paper
seem to us to be appropriate. It will not

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:45.959
be, as it were, a one-off determination but
a process of collaborative working across

00:15:45.959 --> 00:15:52.129
the piece, always with the objective of trying
to improve and enhance the quality of the

00:15:52.129 --> 00:15:57.860
care that we offer to our patients, and joining
much more significantly with local government

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.519
than has been possible so far, even with the
Better Care Fund.

00:16:00.519 --> 00:16:06.110
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think we have also just
discovered that one of David's ex-bosses was

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:07.769
St Luke.

00:16:07.769 --> 00:16:14.399
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: My ex-boss was brilliant
at plagiarism.

